    "problem_statement": "# Duration.__neg__() method doesn't properly negate all components\n\nWhen using the `__neg__()` method on a Duration object, it doesn't properly negate all components of the duration. This causes incorrect behavior when trying to negate a duration.\n\n## Steps to reproduce\n\nCreate a Duration object and try to negate it:\n\n```python\nfrom isodate import Duration\nfrom datetime import timedelta\n\n# Create a duration with years, months and timedelta\nduration = Duration(years=1, months=2, tdelta=timedelta(days=3))\n\n# Negate the duration\nneg_duration = -duration\n\n# Check the values\nprint(f\"Original: years={duration.years}, months={duration.months}, tdelta={duration.tdelta}\")\nprint(f\"Negated: years={neg_duration.years}, months={neg_duration.months}, tdelta={neg_duration.tdelta}\")\n```\n\n## Expected behavior\n\nAll components of the duration should be negated:\n- `years` should be negated\n- `months` should be negated\n- `tdelta` should be negated\n\n## Actual behavior\n\nOnly the `months` component is being negated, while `years` and `tdelta` remain unchanged:\n- `years` is not negated\n- `months` is negated\n- `tdelta` is not negated\n\nThis causes incorrect calculations when using negated durations, especially when formatting them as ISO 8601 strings.\n\n## Additional information\n\nThis also affects the string representation of negated durations. For example:\n\n```python\nfrom isodate import Duration\nfrom datetime import timedelta\n\nduration = Duration(years=1, months=1)\nprint(str(duration))  # Outputs: P1Y1M\nprint(str(-duration))  # Should output: -P1Y1M but doesn't work correctly\n```\n\n</START WRITING>"
